Do not filter /dev/dri entries on d_type when looking for a render node.

DT_UNKNOWN is a legal reply from any filesystem, so requiring DT_CHR is
already fragile on Linux.  On FreeBSD it never matches: /dev/dri/renderD*
are devfs aliases (symlinks, DT_LNK) to the real character devices in
/dev/drm/, and devfs reports DT_LNK for them correctly -- the entry really
is a symlink, so this is not a devfs bug to be worked around here.

The renderD* name match, followed by open() and drmGetVersion() just below,
is complete validation on its own; d_type adds nothing but portability
hazards.

--- src/vrend/vrend_winsys_gbm.c.orig
+++ src/vrend/vrend_winsys_gbm.c
@@ -135,9 +135,12 @@ static int rendernode_open(void)
 
    fd = -1;
    while ((dir_ent = readdir(dir))) {
-      if (dir_ent->d_type != DT_CHR)
-         continue;
-
+      /*
+       * Deliberately no d_type check: DT_UNKNOWN is a legal answer from
+       * any filesystem, and on FreeBSD these entries are devfs aliases
+       * (DT_LNK) to /dev/drm/*.  open() and drmGetVersion() below are
+       * what actually validate the node.
+       */
       if (strncmp(dir_ent->d_name, "renderD", 7))
          continue;
 
